<article class="env-lemma" id="00XR">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00XR">Lemma <span data-tag="00XR">7.21.5</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ be sites. Let $u : \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{D}$ be a functor. Assume that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="(a)"><p>
$u$ is cocontinuous, and 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="(b)"><p>
$u$ is continuous. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Let $g : \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{C}) \to \mathop{\mathit{Sh}}\nolimits (\mathcal{D})$ be the associated morphism of topoi. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
sheafification in the formula $g^{-1} = (u^ p\  )^\# $ is unnecessary, in other words $g^{-1}(\mathcal{G})(U) = \mathcal{G}(u(U))$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$g^{-1}$ has a left adjoint $g_{!} = (u_ p\  )^\# $, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$g^{-1}$ commutes with arbitrary limits and colimits. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By Lemma <a href="/tag/00WW" data-tag="00WW">7.13.2</a> for any sheaf $\mathcal{G}$ on $\mathcal{D}$ the presheaf $u^ p\mathcal{G}$ is a sheaf on $\mathcal{C}$ (and is denoted $u^ s\mathcal{G}$). The functor $\mathcal{F} \mapsto (u_ p\mathcal{F})^\# $ is a left adjoint to $u^ s$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/00WX" data-tag="00WX">7.13.3</a>. The statement on limits and colimits follows from the discussion in Categories, Section <a href="/tag/0036" data-tag="0036">4.24</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>